"""Meetings M32–M43 (Weeks 12–16): the conference block closes, then Studios 11–12.

Week 12 (nb12) — the second week of the D50 **conference block**, rebuilt by
D91 (2026-10-26). The poster went to print at M11, so the week is spoken
delivery: Monday builds the audience analysis and the three timed pitches
(30 s ≤75 words, 90 s ≤200 words, 2 min ≤300 words) for the presentation plan
(M12); Wednesday delivers them under typed adversarial questioning, M12 is due
that night, and M13 opens with a shared folder of every final poster PDF; the
M13 peer review (feedback and questions for every other poster) is due
Thursday. Friday is the PRACTICE PRESENTATIONS studio: each student presents the
printed poster with their M12 pitches while classmates ask the questions from
their M13 reviews, and M14 (the LinkedIn invitation, copied exactly from the
official Expo program) kicks off, due Sunday.

Week 13 (nb13) — the public test: Monday's dress rehearsal (M15 preparation and
the capture plan, one line per visitor's question), the Expo on **Tuesday,
November 17** (required; the M15 photo in front of the poster is due that night,
and the live presentation is scored at the Expo), no class Wednesday, and Friday
is **asynchronous**: M16 part 1, the Conference Reflection Survey. Week 14
(nb14) — the second asynchronous session (Mon Nov 23): M16 part 2, the written
plan to improve and complete the research, and M16 is due that day.

Weeks 15 and 16 are the post-conference studios, and they run in the STANDARD
frame: Monday and Wednesday teach the studio's lessons with their "It is your
turn" work, and Friday IS that studio's milestone. Week 15 (nb15) — **Studio
11, Reproduce and package**: Monday teaches replication and reproduction and you
assemble your package and run it cold yourself ("solo proxy; external cold run
pending"), Wednesday teaches open and reusable packages AND runs the in-class
package exchange (a colleague cold-runs your package from your written
instructions alone while you run theirs), Friday repairs against that run,
clears the two blocking gates moved here from the old poster lock, and makes
the second clean run (M17) — the final milestone of the chain. Week 16 (nb16) —
**Studio 12, Release and direct the next cycle**: Monday teaches Lessons 38–39
and Wednesday teaches Lesson 40. D54 retired the course milestone that used to
close here, so Friday, December 11, is the course-closing reflection session,
collected under participation. The Evidence Defences run inside the Wednesday
peer-defence blocks of Weeks 15 and 16, so the standard frame is never broken to
make room for them.

Course milestone ids under D91: M11 the final poster, the print file (Sun Nov 8,
11:59 PM, terminal); M12 presentation planning (Wed Nov 11); M13 peer review
(Thu Nov 12); M14 the LinkedIn invitation (Sun Nov 15); M15 the Expo
presentation confirmation (Tue Nov 17); M16 the conference reflection and the
plan for the research (Mon Nov 23); M17 the reproducible package and the peer
cold run, Book Milestone 11 (Sun Dec 6) — the last milestone. Week 16 carries
none.

Every in-person Mon/Wed lecture in these weeks opens with the ten-minute LAB
MEETING (D74), and D75 withdrew the assignment inside it: nobody is designated to
report, nothing is prepared, and the instructor asks the room how the projects
are going — what was decided since last time, what the evidence looks like, where
somebody is stuck. Nothing said there is graded, and from minute 10 the
instructor leads the lesson and owns accuracy, the AI tooling, and the clock.
Frames: Monday 10/21/12/7, Wednesday 10/20/12/8, Friday studio 5/40/5. The column
keys are unchanged for compatibility — `srl_slot` now carries the lab-meeting
marker, one identical string on every lecture that has one, and `srl_focus` the
instructor-owned puzzle or challenge that opens the lecture block from minute 10.

Dataset numbers are recomputed from the shipped files: lapop_brazil.csv
(10,000 × 10) and foos_etal.csv (8,375 × 5), both from the rdss package
(Blair, Coppock & Humphreys, MIT License). RDSS chapter titles come from
`planning/RDSS_COVERAGE_MATRIX.md`; never invent a chapter. The AI tool named
in activities is the student's own AI assistant; GenAI Studio appears at its
designated role touchpoints (the Poster Critic at M11, the Reproducibility
Auditor at M17). No fabricated citations anywhere.
"""

